<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate Right Triangle Altitude From Side Lengths</h2>
  <p>The calculator takes three measurements. Leg a is the first perpendicular side, Leg b is the second perpendicular side, and Hypotenuse c is the longest side, opposite the right angle. All three must use the same unit, so convert meters to centimeters or feet to inches before typing.</p>
  <p>Click Calculate Altitude h to see the result with its substitution, and use Reset to clear the fields for the next problem. The calculator answers with the compact formula:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>If you know only two sides, recover the third with the Pythagorean theorem first, or try the general <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-calculator/">altitude calculator</a> for a different starting point.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-altitude -->
<section id="sec-altitude" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Altitude of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>An altitude is the perpendicular distance from a vertex to the opposite side. On this page it always drops from the right-angle vertex straight to the hypotenuse, meeting it at a right angle. The hypotenuse is the base because it is the only side opposite the right angle, and the segment lands inside the triangle.</p>
  <p>A right triangle has three altitudes, but two of them are trivial: leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the altitude to base <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the altitude to base <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, since the legs are already perpendicular. The interesting one is the third altitude, to the hypotenuse, because it is a genuinely new interior segment and it is shorter than either leg.</p>
  <p>That third altitude is what this calculator finds, and it is what textbooks usually mean when they ask for the altitude of a right triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-sides -->
<section id="sec-sides" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Sides of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Correct labels make the formula work. The legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the two perpendicular sides meeting at the right angle. The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the longest side, opposite the right angle. The altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is not a side at all but the perpendicular drop from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>Always identify the hypotenuse first: it is the longest of your three lengths. In a 3-4-5 triangle, 5 must be <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and a quick check <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} /> confirms the labeling. Labeling 5 as a leg would divide by the wrong side and corrupt the answer.</p>
