<!-- Section 5: sec-formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Altitude From Sides Formula</h2>
  <p>The formula is short and easy to memorize:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The legs are multiplied because their product is tied to the area: area equals half of <span set:html={rich('[[ab]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[ab]]')} /> is twice the area. Dividing by the hypotenuse then isolates <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />, since the same area also equals half of <span set:html={rich('[[ch]]')} />. The result is an ordinary length, which is why all three inputs must share one unit.</p>
  <p>Try a 6-8-10 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{6 \\times 8}{10}=\\frac{48}{10}=4.8\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Geometrically, 4.8 cm is the shortest path from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse. (The full proof appears in the proof section below.)</p>
  <p>A useful habit is checking the units before substituting. If <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is 60 mm, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is 8 cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is 100 mm, convert to one unit first: <span set:html={rich('[[a=6\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ cm}]]')} />, which is the same 6-8-10 triangle and gives the same <span set:html={rich('[[4.8\\text{ cm}]]')} />. The formula never repairs mixed units for you, so this one check prevents the most embarrassing errors.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-use -->
<section id="sec-use" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Altitude From Sides Calculator</h2>
  <p>Follow these eight steps for a clean result every time.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the right angle, the corner where two sides meet squarely, often marked with a small square. Without a confirmed right angle, this formula does not apply.</li>
    <li>Locate the two perpendicular legs forming that corner; call them a and b in either order, since the formula treats them symmetrically.</li>
    <li>Identify the hypotenuse: the side opposite the right angle and the longest of the three. Confirm it is longer than both legs.</li>
    <li>Convert all three measurements to the same unit before typing anything. Mixed units are the most common input error.</li>
    <li>Enter a, b, and c into their matching fields.</li>
    <li>Click the Calculate Altitude h button to multiply the legs, divide by the hypotenuse, and display the altitude.</li>
    <li>Read the result and check that the substituted numbers match what you intended to enter.</li>
    <li>Use Reset to clear the inputs before the next problem, so old values cannot corrupt the new answer.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Before trusting an answer, run a Pythagorean check: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2]]')} /> should equal <span set:html={rich('[[c^2]]')} />. Small measurement rounding is fine, but a large mismatch means the sides do not form a right triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-manual -->
<section id="sec-manual" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Altitude Calculation Without a Calculator</h2>
  <p>You can find the altitude by hand in five short steps. Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=9\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=15\\text{ cm}]]')} />, and first confirm the triangle is valid: <span set:html={rich('[[9^2+12^2=81+144=225=15^2]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Step 1, write the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2, substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{9 \\times 12}{15}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3, multiply the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[9 \\times 12=108]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{108}{15}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 4, divide: <span set:html={rich('[[108 \\div 15=7.2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[h=7.2\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Step 5, verify with the area. From the legs, <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\frac{9 \\times 12}{2}=54\\text{ cm}^2]]')} />; from the hypotenuse and altitude, <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\frac{15 \\times 7.2}{2}=54\\text{ cm}^2]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Both areas agree because they describe the same triangle. The legs give one base-height pair and the hypotenuse gives another, but the enclosed region is identical. Matching areas confirm the altitude.</p>
  <p>Why does the area check work so well? Because the altitude formula itself comes from equating the two area expressions, verifying through area closes the loop: any arithmetic slip in the multiplication or division would make the two areas disagree. When they match, both the formula use and the arithmetic are confirmed in one stroke.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-diagram -->
<section id="sec-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Altitude Diagram Explained</h2>
  <p>Label the triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Then AC is leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, BC is leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and AB is the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Point D sits on AB, and segment CD meets AB at a right angle; CD is the altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />. Small square markers at C and D show both right angles.</p>
  <p>The altitude CD splits the big triangle into two smaller right triangles, ACD and BCD, each with its right angle at D. Both are similar to the original triangle ABC and to each other, a fact the proof and projection sections rely on.</p>
  <p>When you sketch your own version, start with the right angle at C, draw the legs, then connect their free ends for the hypotenuse. Drop the altitude last, checking that it meets the hypotenuse squarely. Labeling in the order a, b, c, h keeps every later calculation organized.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-main.svg')} alt="Triangle ABC with right angle at C, altitude CD drawn to hypotenuse AB; AC equals a, BC equals b, AB equals c, CD equals h" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Altitude CD drops from the right angle C to hypotenuse AB; h equals (a x b) / c.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Altitude Formula Work?</h2>
  <p>The formula follows from the area of a triangle. Area equals half the base times the height, and a right triangle offers two convenient base-height pairs. With the legs as base and height:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{a \\times b}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With the hypotenuse as base and the altitude as height:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{c \\times h}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Both expressions describe the same triangle, so set them equal:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{ab}{2}=\\frac{ch}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Multiply by 2, then divide by <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[ab=ch]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>One triangle can be measured with different base-height pairs while its area stays fixed, so equating the two expressions is legitimate and the algebra isolates <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />. The diagram shows the same triangle twice, once with base <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, once with base <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> and height <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The proof also explains why the altitude is always shorter than the legs. Since <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the longest side, dividing <span set:html={rich('[[ab]]')} /> by the largest of the three lengths must produce a value smaller than either leg alone. Every later formula on this page, from projections to similar triangles, is a variation on this one area idea.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-two-base-height.svg')} alt="The same right triangle drawn twice: first with base a and height b, then with base c and height h" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Same triangle, two base-height pairs: area equals ab/2 and ch/2, so h equals ab/c.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Right Triangle Altitude From Sides</h2>
  <p>The seven examples below move from the classic whole-number triples to decimals, equal legs, and mixed units. Each one follows the same rhythm: state what is given, substitute into the formula, simplify, and finish with the unit. Reading them in order builds the habit of checking units and reasonableness before trusting any answer.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Classic 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{3 \\times 4}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}=2.4\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[2.4\\text{ cm}]]')} />. This benchmark example is worth memorizing, since 3-4-5 is the most common Pythagorean triple and 2.4 is a tidy decimal.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{5 \\times 12}{13}=\\frac{60}{13}\\approx 4.615\\text{ m}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The exact altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[60/13]]')} /> meters, about 4.615 m. Keep the fraction when precision matters, since rounding early carries error into follow-up work.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: 8-15-17 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=15\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=17\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{8 \\times 15}{17}=\\frac{120}{17}\\approx 7.059\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude is about <span set:html={rich('[[7.059\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Swapping the legs to 15 and 8 gives the identical product, confirming the formula treats the legs symmetrically.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Equal Legs and the 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=10\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=10\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{10 \\times 10}{10\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}}=5\\sqrt{2}\\approx 7.071\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Equal legs make a 45-45-90 triangle, and the altitude splits it into two congruent halves. The exact form <span set:html={rich('[[5\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> cm beats the decimal in theoretical work.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=1.5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=2\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=2.5\\text{ m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{1.5 \\times 2}{2.5}=\\frac{3}{2.5}=1.2\\text{ m}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[1.2\\text{ m}]]')} />. Decimals need no special treatment: multiply and divide as usual, keeping the same unit on the answer.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Mixed Units That Must Match First</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=30\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=0.4\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=0.5\\text{ m}]]')} />, convert to centimeters first: <span set:html={rich('[[a=30\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=40\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=50\\text{ cm}]]')} />.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{30 \\times 40}{50}=\\frac{1200}{50}=24\\text{ cm}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Converting first gives a clean <span set:html={rich('[[24\\text{ cm}]]')} />. Unconverted meters would have inflated the answer by a factor of 100.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Larger 20-21-29 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=20\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=21\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=29\\text{ ft}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{20 \\times 21}{29}=\\frac{420}{29}\\approx 14.483\\text{ ft}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The altitude is about <span set:html={rich('[[14.483\\text{ ft}]]')} />, comfortably below the shorter leg of 20 ft, as the altitude to the hypotenuse always is.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-345.svg')} alt="Right triangle with legs 3 and 4, hypotenuse 5, and altitude 2.4 drawn to the hypotenuse" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A 3-4-5 triangle drawn to scale: altitude h equals (3 x 4) / 5 = 2.4.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-two-sides -->
<section id="sec-two-sides" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find the Altitude When Only Two Sides Are Known</h2>
  <p>The live calculator asks for all three sides, but two sides are enough to recover the third with the Pythagorean theorem. There are two cases, depending on which sides you know, and both end with the familiar altitude formula.</p>
  <p>The logic is the same in both cases: the Pythagorean theorem restores the missing side, and the altitude formula does the rest. Case 1 adds the legs under a square root to find the hypotenuse, while Case 2 subtracts to recover a leg. Note that Case 2 only works when the known side is truly the hypotenuse; if <span set:html={rich('[[a>c]]')} />, the square root goes negative and no such triangle exists.</p>
  <h3>Case 1: Both Legs Known</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=7]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=24]]')} />. Find the hypotenuse first: <span set:html={rich('[[[c=\\sqrt{7^2+24^2}=\\sqrt{49+576}=\\sqrt{625}=25]]]')} /> Then the altitude follows: <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{7 \\times 24}{25}=\\frac{168}{25}=6.72]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Case 2: One Leg and the Hypotenuse Known</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=8]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=10]]')} />. Recover the missing leg with the rearranged Pythagorean theorem: <span set:html={rich('[[[b=\\sqrt{c^2-a^2}=\\sqrt{100-64}=\\sqrt{36}=6]]]')} /> Then the altitude is: <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{8 \\times 6}{10}=\\frac{48}{10}=4.8]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Treat these as educational alternatives, since the calculator expects three measurements. A dedicated <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">side calculator</a> can recover the missing length before you continue here.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your Altitude Calculation</h2>
  <p>Three independent checks confirm an altitude answer. All three below use the 3-4-5 triangle, where the altitude is 2.4. Use at least one of them whenever an answer will feed into further work, since a wrong altitude quietly corrupts everything built on it.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Area Comparison</h3>
  <p>Compute the area both ways and require agreement. From the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{3 \\times 4}{2}=6]]]')} /> From the hypotenuse and altitude: <span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{5 \\times 2.4}{2}=6]]]')} /> Matching areas confirm the altitude itself.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Hypotenuse Projections</h3>
  <p>Split the hypotenuse into the leg projections <span set:html={rich('[[p=\\frac{a^2}{c}=\\frac{9}{5}=1.8]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[q=\\frac{b^2}{c}=\\frac{16}{5}=3.2]]')} />. The altitude is their geometric mean: <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{1.8 \\times 3.2}=\\sqrt{5.76}=2.4]]]')} /> Recovering 2.4 from a different formula is strong evidence.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Pythagorean Check</h3>
  <p>Confirm the sides form a right triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[[3^2+4^2=9+16=25=5^2]]]')} /> This validates the side lengths, not the altitude. Run it first when a problem hands you the sides, then use the area or projection check to validate the altitude.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-vs-projections -->
<section id="sec-vs-projections" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Altitude From Sides vs. Altitude From Projections</h2>
  <p>The altitude can be found two ways. The side method on this page is <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} />, starting from the three side lengths. The projection method is <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]')} />, starting from the two segments into which the altitude divides the hypotenuse. Both describe the same segment and always agree for the same triangle; the choice depends on which measurements you have. For the second route, see the <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-projections-calculator/">altitude-from-projections calculator</a> page.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Method</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Required inputs</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Formula</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Best use case</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Altitude from sides</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Legs a and b, hypotenuse c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">h = ab / c</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Side lengths are known; no square roots needed</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Altitude from projections</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Hypotenuse segments p and q</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">h = square root of (p times q)</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">The altitude foot divides the hypotenuse into known parts</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-projections-similar.svg')} alt="Right triangle with altitude h showing projections p and q and the two smaller similar triangles" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The altitude splits the hypotenuse into p and q; the side formula and the projection formula give the same h.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-area-rel -->
<section id="sec-area-rel" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Altitude, Area, and Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>Altitude, area, and hypotenuse link through two expressions for one area. From the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{ab}{2}]]]')} /> From the hypotenuse and altitude: <span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{ch}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Solving the second expression for <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> gives a handy rearrangement: <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{2A}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>This offers a second route when the area is known directly. For a triangle with area <span set:html={rich('[[54\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[15\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{2 \\times 54}{15}=\\frac{108}{15}=7.2\\text{ cm}]]]')} />, the same answer the side formula gave for the 9-12-15 triangle. If you need the area first, an <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">area calculator</a> handles that step, and the <a href="/right-triangle-area-from-altitude-calculator/">area-from-altitude calculator</a> reverses the computation once <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is known.</p>
  <p>This route shines when a problem states the area directly, as many textbook questions do, or when the area was computed in a previous part. Instead of hunting for the individual legs, you divide twice the area by the hypotenuse and finish in one line. It is the same altitude the side formula gives, reached from a different starting point.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Special Properties of the Altitude to the Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>The altitude to the hypotenuse is always shorter than either leg and shorter than the hypotenuse itself, since <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} /> falls below <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> whenever <span set:html={rich('[[b<c]]')} />. Equal legs give a symmetric 45-45-90 triangle in which the altitude bisects the hypotenuse. For a fixed hypotenuse, the altitude is greatest when the legs are equal: with hypotenuse 10, the 45-45-90 triangle gives <span set:html={rich('[[h=5]]')} /> while the 6-8-10 triangle gives only 4.8.</p>
  <p>No split of the same hypotenuse beats that maximum, which is the inequality <span set:html={rich('[[h \\le \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, with equality only in the 45-45-90 case. Scaling also behaves simply: doubling every side doubles the altitude, so 3-4-5 at 2.4 becomes 6-8-10 at 4.8. Finally, the altitude divides the triangle into two smaller triangles similar to the original, which is why the projection and area relationships all agree.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-equal-vs-unequal.svg')} alt="Two right triangles sharing a hypotenuse of 10: a 45-45-90 triangle with altitude 5 and a 6-8-10 triangle with altitude 4.8" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Same hypotenuse 10: equal legs give the tallest altitude, 5.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Altitude</h2>
  <p>Avoid these nine errors and nearly every altitude calculation goes smoothly.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confusing the hypotenuse with a leg. Fix: pick the longest side as c first; in a 3-4-5 triangle, 5 must be c.</li>
    <li>Multiplying all three sides. Computing abc has no geometric meaning here. Fix: multiply only the legs, then divide by the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to divide by c. Stopping at ab leaves twice the area, not a length. Fix: always finish with the division.</li>
    <li>Mixing units. Entering a in centimeters and c in meters corrupts the division silently. Fix: convert everything to one unit first.</li>
    <li>Using sides that do not form a right triangle. The formula assumes a right angle, so 2, 3, 4 gives nonsense. Fix: check a^2 + b^2 = c^2 first.</li>
    <li>Confusing the altitude with one of the legs. Textbooks asking for the altitude usually mean the one to the hypotenuse. Fix: confirm which altitude the question wants.</li>
    <li>Rounding intermediate values too early. Rounding 60/13 to 4.6 mid-calculation injects error. Fix: round only the final answer.</li>
    <li>Using an area formula incorrectly. Writing Area = c times h without the half doubles the answer. Fix: use Area = ch/2 when solving for h.</li>
    <li>Confusing altitude to the hypotenuse with height relative to a leg. These are different segments. Fix: the altitude to the hypotenuse is the shorter interior segment h = ab/c.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Uses of Right Triangle Altitude Calculations</h2>
  <p>Students meet this altitude in geometry homework on area, similarity, and the geometric mean, while teachers use it to connect the Pythagorean theorem with area formulas in one picture, since a single diagram carries both <span set:html={rich('[[ab/2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[ch/2]]')} />. Drafters and designers use it when a triangular panel or bracket must be dimensioned from its altitude, and triangular bracing layouts sometimes need the shortest cross distance between a joint and a member.</p>
  <p>Surveyors working with right-triangle sight lines use the same relationship to check computed distances against measured ones. Treat these as mathematical models with idealized measurements: real engineering or construction work needs suitable measurement methods, stated design assumptions, and professional standards before any formula result is trusted on site.</p>
  <p>In the classroom the altitude also serves as a bridge topic: it ties the Pythagorean theorem, area formulas, and similar triangles together in one figure. A single well-drawn diagram can carry a whole lesson, which is why this construction appears so often in textbooks.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Solutions</h2>
  <p>Try each problem on your own, then check the worked answers below.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Basic Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs of 6 cm and 8 cm and a hypotenuse of 10 cm. Find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Pythagorean Triple</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs of 12 m and 16 m and a hypotenuse of 20 m. Find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs of 4.5 in and 6 in and a hypotenuse of 7.5 in. Find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Equal Legs</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has two equal legs of 14 ft each. Find the hypotenuse and then the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Unit Conversion</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has sides of 2.4 m, 70 cm, and 2.5 m. Convert units first, then find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>1. <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{6 \\times 8}{10}=\\frac{48}{10}=4.8\\text{ cm}]]')} />. The scaled 3-4-5 pattern gives a clean decimal.</p>
  <p>2. <span set:html={rich('[[12^2+16^2=144+256=400=20^2]]')} /> confirms the triple, then <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{12 \\times 16}{20}=\\frac{192}{20}=9.6\\text{ m}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>3. <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{4.5 \\times 6}{7.5}=\\frac{27}{7.5}=3.6\\text{ in}]]')} />. Decimals divide neatly when the triple is consistent.</p>
  <p>4. <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{14^2+14^2}=14\\sqrt{2}\\text{ ft}]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{14 \\times 14}{14\\sqrt{2}}=7\\sqrt{2}\\approx 9.899\\text{ ft}]]')} />, exactly half the hypotenuse as expected for 45-45-90.</p>
  <p>5. In centimeters: <span set:html={rich('[[a=240]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=70]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=250]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[2.4^2+0.7^2=5.76+0.49=6.25=2.5^2]]')} /> checks out. Then <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{240 \\times 70}{250}=\\frac{16800}{250}=67.2\\text{ cm}]]')} />.</p>
</section>

